# Hostile audit — all-orders train phasor / straight-ray rigidity and minimal-`K_5` reciprocal reduction

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Audited sources:**
- `research/target_c_train_closure_all_orders_phasor_straight_ray_2026-09-18.md`
- `research/target_c_one_anchor_k5_reciprocal_unit_force_2026-09-18.md`
- `research/target_c_one_anchor_k5_reciprocal_turn_gap_2026-09-18.md`

**Status:** internal hostile audit; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Verdict:** PASS at the stated proof-only scopes.  C-II remains open.

The audit focused on sign conventions, hidden use of the dual-kernel hypothesis,
finite-versus-infinitesimal integration, degeneracies in the five-face reciprocal
construction, and the transfer of physical pentagon geometry to reciprocal angles.

---

## 1. The all-orders phasor identity uses no false `Jy` kernel assumption

For a dual mode `y in ker C^T`, the already established train coefficient satisfies

\[
v_\tau(y)=\lambda_\tau u_\tau.
\]

If `Psi(theta)=0`, contraction with any block test vector vanishes.  Therefore both
`y` and `Jy` may be used as test vectors even though `Jy` need not lie in `ker C^T`.
The two contractions are exactly

\[
\sum_\tau\lambda_\tau\cos\theta_\tau=0,
\qquad
\sum_\tau\lambda_\tau\sin\theta_\tau=0.
\]

Thus

\[
\sum_\tau\lambda_\tau e^{i\theta_\tau}=0
\]

is exact.  No symmetry or omitted outer-face equation is required.  Outer--outer trains
have `lambda=0` automatically.

The singleton-sign corollary is also exact: after using `sum lambda=0`, one side of the
phasor identity is a convex combination of unit complex numbers with modulus one.
Equality in the triangle inequality forces all nonzero-support phases to coincide.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 2. The straight-ray criterion is an equivalence, not just a necessary condition

For one face,

\[
\Psi_F(tx)=\sum_a S_{F,a}e^{ita}.
\]

Distinct real frequencies give an invertible Vandermonde system after differentiating
orders `0,...,m-1` at zero.  Hence exact vanishing on an interval forces every slope
group sum `S_{F,a}` to vanish separately.  The converse is immediate.

This argument is face-local and does not invoke a genericity assumption on the speeds.
Repeated speeds are intentionally grouped before the Vandermonde step.

On a bounded contact pentagon, a proper zero-sum side subset is impossible because a
two-side zero sum would be a forbidden parallel pair, while a three-side zero sum has
a complementary two-side zero sum.  Therefore every exact straight ray has one common
speed on all five train occurrences of each pentagon.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 3. Scope correction for equal-magnitude rays

If `s in ker C` and every `s_tau` is `+1` or `-1`, then

\[
\Psi(ts)=\cos t\,\Psi(0)+\sin t\,Cs=0
\]

for every real `t`.  This is an exact statement about the train-angle face-closure map.

For the source geometry, the existing train-angle reconstruction theorem turns a
sufficiently small neighborhood of exact face closures into actual contact-preserving
placements; strict noncontact slack persists only locally.  The new note should not be
read as claiming that the physical source remains feasible for arbitrary large `t`.
Its load-bearing conclusion is local finite integration, which is valid.

The pentagon result then forces an equal-magnitude ray to have one common sign on every
connected pentagon-train component.  This does not say that arbitrary independent
component rotations solve superpentagon equations; the note only states that every
actual straight ray has this coordinate normal form.

**Disposition:** PASS with the stated local-realization scope.

---

## 4. The `6(Q-f)` support bound is only an incidence bound

Let `S` be the superpentagonal bounded faces and

\[
X=Q-f=\sum_F(\deg F-5).
\]

Then `|S|<=X` and

\[
\sum_{F\in S}\deg F\le5X+X=6X.
\]

Every non-outer--outer train with no pentagonal bounded endpoint is incident to at least
one face in `S`, so the number of such distinct trains is at most that incidence sum.
This proves the claimed support bound.

No dimension of the nonlinear zero set is inferred from `6X`; a train with two
superpentagonal endpoints is merely double-counted in an upper bound.  In the current
proof-only live window `X/D<0.000141+o(1)`, giving the safe support constant `0.000846`.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 5. The minimal `K_5` reciprocal construction has no hidden zero-length edge

The reciprocal note is scoped to the degree-pure equality object: five one-anchor
pentagons, relation graph `K_5`, and a nonzero dual mode on that component.  Each face
therefore has exactly its one outside/activated anchor and its four `K_5` internal
trains.

Every dual face vector is nonzero by relation-component propagation.  If two dual
vectors coincide while a third differs, that third face sees two parallel internal
train directions, contradicting the no-parallel pentagon theorem.  If any two coincide,
the only remaining possibility is that all five coincide.  Then all five anchors are
projectively parallel; summing the five physical pentagon closure equations cancels the
ten internal trains and leaves an odd sum of five parallel unit anchor vectors, which
cannot vanish.  Thus all five dual vectors are distinct.

The same no-parallel theorem excludes every collinear triple among the five dual points
and the common zero point.  Therefore all fifteen reciprocal edge differences are
nonzero and the six reciprocal points affinely span the plane.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 6. Occurrence signs give a genuine symmetric signed length on each reciprocal edge

For an internal train `ij`, if its occurrence sign at `F_i` is `epsilon_ij`, then

\[
\epsilon_{ij}(y_i-y_j)=\lambda_{ij}u_{ij}.
\]

The physical oriented side at `F_i` is `epsilon_ij u_ij`, so

\[
y_i-y_j=\lambda_{ij}w_{ij}^{(i)}.
\]

At `F_j` both the reciprocal difference and the oriented side reverse, leaving the
same scalar `lambda_ij`.  Since `w_ij` is unit,

\[
|\lambda_{ij}|=|y_i-y_j|.
\]

The same calculation applies to an anchor after setting the zero dual endpoint to
`y_0=0`.

Thus `omega_ij=1/lambda_ij` is a well-defined symmetric signed inverse-distance stress,
not an artifact of coherent train orientation.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 7. Ground equilibrium and the tensor identity are exact

The five non-ground reciprocal equilibrium equations are exactly the five physical
pentagon closure equations.  Summing them cancels each internal train once with each
orientation.  The five remaining anchor terms sum to zero, which is precisely the
reciprocal equilibrium equation at `y_0` after reversing sign.

For the stress matrix `Omega`, equilibrium gives

\[
\Omega\mathbf1=\Omega Y_x=\Omega Y_y=0.
\]

Affine spanning makes these three kernel vectors independent, so `rank Omega<=3`.
No claim of equality is made.

For the tensor identity, pair the endpoint contributions after taking the outer product
of `y_i` with its equilibrium equation.  Edge `ij` contributes

\[
{(y_i-y_j)(y_i-y_j)^T\over\lambda_{ij}},
\]

so the total is zero.  Taking the trace gives

\[
\sum_{i<j}{|y_i-y_j|^2\over\lambda_{ij}}
=\sum_{i<j}\lambda_{ij}=0,
\]

because `lambda_ij^2=|y_i-y_j|^2`.  The positive/negative length balance follows.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 8. The reciprocal 120-degree gap is source-derived and strict

At a physical pentagon, two consecutive unit sides `u,v` have an across-the-vertex
chord `u+v`.  Source separation gives `|u+v|>=1`; equality would make that chord an
additional unit contact strictly inside the convex face, impossible because the unit
contact graph is complete.  Hence `|u+v|>1`.

If `theta` is the exterior turn,

\[
2\cos(\theta/2)>1,
\]

so `theta<2pi/3`.  The five reciprocal force vectors at each non-ground point are
exactly these five oriented pentagon sides, hence every cyclic force-direction gap is
strictly below 120 degrees.

At a non-ground reciprocal hull vertex with interior angle `phi`, the unsigned edge
directions occupy a cone of width `phi`; sign flips place force directions in that
cone or its opposite.  The two complementary empty angular intervals each have width
`pi-phi`.  Since no cyclic empty gap reaches `2pi/3`, one gets `phi>pi/3`.

The only stated order-type consequence is that a triangular reciprocal hull must
contain `y_0`; that follows because three non-ground hull angles could not all exceed
60 degrees.

**Disposition:** PASS.

---

## 9. What remains unproved

None of these reductions closes C-II.

The all-orders phasor law does not by itself integrate arbitrary singular tangent
vectors.  The straight-ray theorem rules out a broad affine-angle mechanism on the
pentagonal bulk but leaves genuinely curved train-angle paths.  The reciprocal theorem
does not yet exclude the signed six-point unit-force equilibrium; it only replaces the
minimal `K_5` relation block by a smaller metric/algebraic target with strict inherited
angle constraints.

The next concrete finite attack is therefore to classify the reciprocal `K_6` system:

\[
\sum_{j\ne i}s_{ij}{y_i-y_j\over|y_i-y_j|}=0
\qquad(i=0,\ldots,5),
\]

with no three reciprocal points collinear and with every non-ground signed direction
set having cyclic gaps below 120 degrees.  Proving this system impossible would remove
the sharp five-face dual block.  A surviving exact model would instead be a useful
regression and would identify the geometry that any stronger corank theorem has to
handle.

**Audit disposition:** PASS.  The three new notes are proof-only candidates and do not
alter the authoritative theorem state.